## Module 3: Blast Design & Rock Fragmentation Geometry## 1. Core Geometric Parameters
Designing a blast pattern requires precise spatial math to control the direction of broken rock and minimize flyrock:

      [ Free Face / Open Air ]
 ____________

|   <---  Burden (B)  --->   |     |
|                            O     O <-- Blast Holes

|   <----- Spacing (S) ----->      |
|____________|


* Burden (B): The shortest distance from a drill hole to the nearest open, free rock face.
* Spacing (S): The distance between individual drill holes in the same row.
* Sub-drilling (J): The depth a hole is drilled below the planned floor level. This ensures that the bottom of the rock breaks cleanly and doesn't leave dangerous, uneven rock ridges ("toes") on the floor.
* Stemming (H): Inert material (like crushed stone or gravel) packed into the top part of the drill hole. It plugs the hole so expanding gases don't shoot straight out into the air like a cannon.

## 2. Engineering Formulas

* Powder Factor (PF): Measures blasting efficiency.
$$\text{Powder Factor} = \frac{\text{Total Weight of Explosive Used (kg)}}{\text{Volume of Rock Broken (m}^3\text{ or tons)}}$$ 
* Approximating Burden (Ash's Formula): A standard guideline where the burden is typically 20 to 30 times the diameter of the drill hole ($D$).
$$B \approx 25 \times D$$ 

